WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.181 --> 00:00:14.805
I’m Frank Schwach. 
I'm a computational biologist in the Plasmogen team, 

00:00:14.805 --> 00:00:19.048
which is part of the Malaria programme at the 
Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute.

00:00:19.048 --> 00:00:26.387
I use computer programming to design DNA vectors
 that can manipulate genes in malaria parasites.

00:00:26.387 --> 00:00:34.830
A vector is a DNA molecule that we use to manipulate
 the genome of the parasite in a specific way. 

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:43.399
We can delete a gene. We can modify a gene to do something else.
We can change the way it's being used by the parasite. 

00:00:43.399 --> 00:00:48.274
When we knockout a gene we don't just delete the gene
 we need to replace it with something.

00:00:48.274 --> 00:00:57.989
We bring in a little package of DNA that conveys
 new functions to the mutant cell. We call this a resistance marker. 

00:00:58.031 --> 00:01:08.595
The resistance markers are guided into the right place in the 
malaria genome by short DNA sequences called oligonucleotides. 

00:01:08.595 --> 00:01:15.976
The design software identifies the best oligonucleotides
 for each individual gene.

00:01:15.976 --> 00:01:23.496
Once the software has designed oligonucleotides
it will produce order sheets for them 

00:01:23.496 --> 00:01:28.926
which will be sent to external companies to actually produce
 the oligonucleotides.

00:01:28.926 --> 00:01:35.043
Once we receive these custom-made oligonucleotides,
 the lab based stage of the process begins. 

00:01:35.043 --> 00:01:38.437
This has many steps and takes about 2 weeks.

00:01:38.437 --> 00:01:47.134
Firstly the oligonucleotides are mixed with template DNA
 and a PCR reaction is performed.

00:01:47.134 --> 00:01:54.643
Now each well contains DNA fragments with the same resistance 
marker genes - known as a resistance cassette -

00:01:54.643 --> 00:02:02.916
but the ends of these fragments differ and those will later guide 
the finished vector to its specific target gene in the parasite.

00:02:02.916 --> 00:02:06.744
These ends are the oligonucleotides we designed earlier. 

00:02:06.744 --> 00:02:12.047
Next a crucial step called recombineering happens in bacteria.

00:02:12.047 --> 00:02:17.986
Each of these bacterial cultures harbour a specific 
short piece of malaria DNA 

00:02:17.986 --> 00:02:22.738
that contains an individual target gene 
identified at the design stage. 

00:02:22.738 --> 00:02:32.792
The product of the earlier PCR reaction is then shot into these 
bacterial cells using an electroporation machine. 

00:02:32.792 --> 00:02:38.850
Under specific culture conditions the cells now perform
 the recombineering reaction, 

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:43.559
which replaces a piece of malaria DNA with a resistance cassette. 

00:02:43.559 --> 00:02:47.547
When exposed to antibiotics, due to the resistance marker, 

00:02:47.547 --> 00:02:52.977
only the bacterial cells that have performed 
this reaction correctly will survive.

00:02:52.977 --> 00:02:59.425
We now need to replace the bacterial resistance genes
 for a new malaria specific cassette 

00:02:59.425 --> 00:03:04.898
that will allow us to eventually select for successful 
vectors in the Malaria parasite.

00:03:04.898 --> 00:03:11.601
For this, we first extract the modified DNA – known as plasmids -  
from the selected bacteria. 

00:03:11.601 --> 00:03:17.669
We then perform a so-called gateway reaction, 
to swap one cassette for another. 

00:03:27.384 --> 00:03:36.378
The modified plasmid is put back into bacteria using
 electroporation again and then plated up. 

00:03:37.905 --> 00:03:43.802
Once the bacteria have grown on the plates we pick the colonies 
 and culture them overnight.

00:03:43.802 --> 00:03:52.470
As a first quality control step we run another PCR reaction to 
confirm that the genomic fragment is correctly modified.

00:03:52.470 --> 00:04:00.064
We then run the products on a gel and record 
the results for our sample tracking database. 

00:04:00.616 --> 00:04:06.640
Once we know the DNA modification has worked 
the bacteria are stored in freezers.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:14.064
The bacteria now contain the finals vectors that are
 ready to modify the genome of Malaria parasites. 

00:04:14.064 --> 00:04:19.537
We make these available to a worldwide community of
 scientists through our website 

00:04:19.537 --> 00:04:23.567
and also use them for large scale experiments of our own.

00:04:23.567 --> 00:04:29.252
Working at scale is very different from performing a few
 individual reactions in the lab, 

00:04:29.252 --> 00:04:33.918
even at the relatively modest scale of the small Malaria genome.

00:04:33.918 --> 00:04:41.555
Procedures need to be highly optimised and standardized
 and you need to think about tracking the process through the lab. 

00:04:41.555 --> 00:04:44.991
Good communication between team members is very important.

00:04:44.991 --> 00:04:52.364
When you produce at scale any mistakes made also quickly 
multiply and may cost a lot of time and money to rectify. 

00:04:52.364 --> 00:04:57.412
I joined the project at an early stage when it wasn't
 a scaled-up pipeline yet;

00:04:57.412 --> 00:05:02.715
it was great to see the progress from a few bespoke
 constructs to a sizeable scale.

00:05:02.715 --> 00:05:06.876
We recently published  an important piece of work
that probes half of the genome 

00:05:06.876 --> 00:05:15.742
for the role of genes and growth of the parasite in the blood and it's 
great to see all that work, culminating in a new scientific discovery. 

